  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 1205 AM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nMonday night, we remain very mild with lows in the low to mid 70s\nareawide. Likewise, moisture will remain across the area and \nhighs should be a couple degrees warmer for Tuesday and at least \nWednesday as we remain influenced by the subtropical ridge. Thus, \nexpect heat indices to soar into the 100-108 degree range for the \nearly start to next week. We'll need to monitor this closely as \nit may approach heat advisory criteria, if this pattern holds. \nAdditionally, we could see isolated showers and storms across the \nCoastal Plains Tuesday afternoon and again for Wednesday afternoon\nas plenty of moisture remains over the area along with the return\nof our typical seabreeze allowing storms to from as far north as \nthe I-35 Corridor. \n\nBy late week, we should begin to see the pattern change once more\nas our subtropical ridge moves off to the east and the upper \ntrough moves over the Rockies and into the Coastal Plains. This in\nturn drives a cold front down through our area. Global models \nhave done fairly poorly so far on the timing and strength of this \nfront so it remains to be seen just how much cooler we get behind \nit and how much moisture we can wring out of it. As of right now \neach model run keeps coming in drier and drier so have went with a\nblend of temps and pops until we get more model consistence on \nhow this pattern unfolds. Some models bring in significantly \ncolder air behind this front while others are not as cool and \nthus less of an impact to our area. Continue to check back as the \nforecast becomes clearer over the coming days.",
